WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:04.170
In this lesson, we're going to start
with the email agent

2
00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:05.939
that we created previously.

3
00:00:05.939 --> 00:00:09.776
And we're going to add in tools
to allow it to operate on semantic memory.

4
00:00:10.543 --> 00:00:14.914
Now as a reminder, semantic memory
normally stores facts about the user.

5
00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:19.019
So we're going to use these tools
to learn facts about users.

6
00:00:19.386 --> 00:00:22.222
We're then going to store them
in a long-term memory store.

7
00:00:22.222 --> 00:00:25.325
And then we're going to add a separate
tool to search over these facts.

8
00:00:25.892 --> 00:00:27.660
Let's see it in code.

9
00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:30.563
So here we have a lot of set up
from the previous lesson.

10
00:00:30.563 --> 00:00:32.098
And this should look all the same.

11
00:00:32.098 --> 00:00:33.967
We're going to load some environment
variables.

12
00:00:33.967 --> 00:00:35.602
We're going to define our profile.

13
00:00:35.602 --> 00:00:37.937
We're going to define
our prompt instructions.

14
00:00:37.937 --> 00:00:39.472
An example email.

15
00:00:39.472 --> 00:00:40.907
We're going to set up the router.

16
00:00:40.907 --> 00:00:42.942
Nothing's changed here.

17
00:00:42.942 --> 00:00:44.511
We're going to import some prompts.

18
00:00:44.511 --> 00:00:47.480
We're going to define our three
tools that we had before.

19
00:00:50.116 --> 00:00:50.884
Now we're going to

20
00:00:50.884 --> 00:00:53.887
start to define our tools
for managing memory.

21
00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:55.522
The first thing we're going to want to do

22
00:00:55.522 --> 00:00:58.525
is properly set up our long-term
memory store.

23
00:00:59.359 --> 00:01:02.362
And so for this lesson
we're just going to use an in-memory store

24
00:01:02.362 --> 00:01:04.597
a store
that will store everything in memory.

25
00:01:04.597 --> 00:01:07.600
And so we're going to import
that from LangGraph.

26
00:01:10.703 --> 00:01:11.704
When we initialize

27
00:01:11.704 --> 00:01:16.042
it, we're going to want to make sure
that we pass in this index param.

28
00:01:16.643 --> 00:01:19.179
Here we're passing in an embedding model

29
00:01:19.179 --> 00:01:22.215
to use
to index the memories that we store.

30
00:01:22.849 --> 00:01:25.251
So we're going to use the OpenAI
text embedding

31
00:01:25.251 --> 00:01:28.254
three small model.

32
00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:30.924
Afterwards,

33
00:01:30.924 --> 00:01:33.927
we're going to import two functions
from Langmem.

34
00:01:33.993 --> 00:01:38.031
Langmem is a wrapper on top of LangGraph
for working with memory.

35
00:01:38.865 --> 00:01:41.601
These two functions are going to be called
create manage

36
00:01:41.601 --> 00:01:44.604
memory tool and create search memory tool.

37
00:01:44.671 --> 00:01:49.042
And these will be tools
for managing and searching over memories.

38
00:01:50.143 --> 00:01:51.077
To create them,

39
00:01:51.077 --> 00:01:52.378
it's pretty simple.

40
00:01:52.378 --> 00:01:55.315
We're just going to define the namespace
that we want

41
00:01:55.315 --> 00:01:58.318
these tools to operate on.

42
00:01:59.486 --> 00:02:01.087
So the namespace that we're going to use

43
00:02:01.087 --> 00:02:04.224
for both is email assistant
as the first value.

44
00:02:04.924 --> 00:02:08.928
Then this templated LangGraph user ID
and I'll come back to that

45
00:02:08.928 --> 00:02:12.165
as a second value
and then collection as the third value.

46
00:02:12.999 --> 00:02:15.201
So what exactly is this?

47
00:02:15.201 --> 00:02:18.171
When we invoke the agent
now we're going to pass in a LangGraph

48
00:02:18.171 --> 00:02:21.708
user ID as part of the runtime
configuration.

49
00:02:22.208 --> 00:02:25.445
And that's going to be use to namespace
this memory.

50
00:02:26.546 --> 00:02:28.748
That way if we're dealing with emails

51
00:02:28.748 --> 00:02:31.818
from multiple users, we can pretty easily

52
00:02:32.152 --> 00:02:35.922
just have different collections
of memories for different users.

53
00:02:35.955 --> 00:02:37.857
We wouldn't want them to be the same.

54
00:02:37.857 --> 00:02:39.425
And so here's where we define that

55
00:02:39.425 --> 00:02:40.460
we store them separately

56
00:02:41.694 --> 00:02:44.130
because these are just LangChain tools,

57
00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:47.133
we can inspect them
and see what attributes they have.

58
00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:50.870
So they should have a name
so we can see that manage

59
00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:53.873
memory tools name is called manage memory.

60
00:02:54.107 --> 00:02:56.709
They should also
probably have a description.

61
00:02:56.709 --> 00:03:00.146
So we can see that this is the description
of how to use the tool.

62
00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:03.683
Let's print it out
to make it a little bit more legible.

63
00:03:04.551 --> 00:03:06.719
Here, we can see that
it contains instructions

64
00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:09.722
for creating, updating or deleting

65
00:03:09.756 --> 00:03:12.759
persistent memories.

66
00:03:14.827 --> 00:03:17.830
We can also take a look
at the arguments to this tool.

67
00:03:18.064 --> 00:03:21.000
We can see that it takes in content,

68
00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:22.302
then an action.

69
00:03:22.302 --> 00:03:26.906
So the action can be create,
update or delete and then an ID.

70
00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:31.911
And so basically what's happening is
these memories are managed by IDs.

71
00:03:32.212 --> 00:03:37.212
And so when we call to this tool
we can pass in the ID of a memory,

72
00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:39.652
the action we want to take on it

73
00:03:39.652 --> 00:03:42.755
or whether we should update it
or create it or delete it,

74
00:03:43.623 --> 00:03:46.626
and then the content of that memory
as well.

75
00:03:48.661 --> 00:03:52.232
If we look at the search memory tool,
we can see that's called search memory.

76
00:03:52.765 --> 00:03:53.967
The description is pretty simple.

77
00:03:53.967 --> 00:03:55.535
It's just search your long-term memories

78
00:03:55.535 --> 00:03:58.538
for information
relevant to your current context.

79
00:04:00.039 --> 00:04:02.041
And the arguments are also pretty simple.

80
00:04:02.041 --> 00:04:05.545
It's just a query,
a limit for the number of memories

81
00:04:05.545 --> 00:04:08.948
that we want to return, some offset,
and then some filter

82
00:04:08.948 --> 00:04:11.951
if we want.

83
00:04:12.986 --> 00:04:15.955
Let's now go on to define the agent. Here,

84
00:04:15.955 --> 00:04:18.625
we're
going to take the agent system prompt

85
00:04:18.625 --> 00:04:19.892
and we're actually going to modify it

86
00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:23.329
a little bit because now we need to
add in these two extra tools.

87
00:04:24.397 --> 00:04:26.032
So this is slightly modified

88
00:04:26.032 --> 00:04:29.002
from the previous lesson.

89
00:04:29.902 --> 00:04:32.872
We're still going to have
the similar create prompt function,

90
00:04:33.139 --> 00:04:36.309
which basically creates
this same message and appends it

91
00:04:36.342 --> 00:04:39.312
before the list of messages in the state.

92
00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:43.583
After that,
we can build our agent as normal.

93
00:04:43.716 --> 00:04:46.719
We'll import this create react agent
function.

94
00:04:47.353 --> 00:04:50.089
We'll create our list of tools
which now contains

95
00:04:50.089 --> 00:04:53.092
this manage memory tool
in the search memory tool.

96
00:04:53.326 --> 00:04:57.297
And then we'll create our react agent
which we'll now call response agent.

97
00:04:57.597 --> 00:05:00.566
And notice
that we're also passing in the store

98
00:05:00.566 --> 00:05:03.569
to ensure
that this is passed to the agent.

99
00:05:04.304 --> 00:05:06.506
Let's now test the agent.

100
00:05:06.506 --> 00:05:10.743
The first thing we're going to want to do
is actually define a config

101
00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:14.480
that we're going to pass to the agent
with some runtime configuration.

102
00:05:14.747 --> 00:05:17.450
And it's going to have to
have this LangGraph user ID.

103
00:05:18.584 --> 00:05:19.485
So let's create that

104
00:05:19.485 --> 00:05:22.555
first. You can change this value
which I'm calling lance

105
00:05:22.789 --> 00:05:25.792
to be anything you want.

106
00:05:27.193 --> 00:05:27.960
Let's now call it

107
00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:31.397
with a message that has some information
that we should probably remember.

108
00:05:31.397 --> 00:05:33.333
Like Jim is my friend.

109
00:05:33.333 --> 00:05:35.802
And notice here I'm passing in the config

110
00:05:35.802 --> 00:05:38.805
as a second parameter.

111
00:05:41.107 --> 00:05:42.775
Let's take a look at the response.

112
00:05:42.775 --> 00:05:46.045
If we print out the list of messages,
we can see that we have this human message

113
00:05:46.045 --> 00:05:47.380
which we pass in.

114
00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:49.215
And then we have this AI message.

115
00:05:49.215 --> 00:05:54.020
It calls the manage memory tool
with the arguments content.

116
00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:56.889
Jim is John Doe's friend.

117
00:05:56.889 --> 00:05:59.058
Notice
that we're not specifying an action,

118
00:05:59.058 --> 00:06:01.260
because the default is to create,

119
00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:05.131
and we're not specifying an ID
because it will create one by default.

120
00:06:06.833 --> 00:06:07.233
We get

121
00:06:07.233 --> 00:06:10.336
back a message that we created this memory
with this user ID.

122
00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:14.307
And then the final message is
that it's just recorded this information

123
00:06:14.307 --> 00:06:17.310
about Jim.

124
00:06:18.144 --> 00:06:20.012
Let's try this with another input.

125
00:06:20.012 --> 00:06:22.448
Let's ask now who is Jim?

126
00:06:22.448 --> 00:06:25.284
And notice
that we're passing in the same config

127
00:06:25.284 --> 00:06:28.287
as before.

128
00:06:29.722 --> 00:06:30.356
Let's take a look

129
00:06:30.356 --> 00:06:33.359
at the list of messages
that we get back as a response.

130
00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:36.295
Who is Jim? This is our user message.

131
00:06:36.295 --> 00:06:39.766
We then get a message from the AI
which calls a tool.

132
00:06:39.766 --> 00:06:43.403
It calls this search memory tool
with a query parameter of Jim.

133
00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:47.140
We get back this list of messages.

134
00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:49.375
In this case, we only have one message,

135
00:06:49.375 --> 00:06:52.779
which is this namespace memory
at this value.

136
00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:58.317
This key is the ID, and then the content
is Jim is John Doe's friend.

137
00:07:00.019 --> 00:07:00.720
And then we get back

138
00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:04.490
a response from the AI message
that summarizes the above interaction.

139
00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:07.627
So it says, based on my memory search,
I can see that Jim is John Doe's friend.

140
00:07:13.466 --> 00:07:15.101
If we want to inspect the store

141
00:07:15.101 --> 00:07:18.838
directly, we can.
We can call list namespaces,

142
00:07:18.838 --> 00:07:21.841
and we can see that this one namespace
exists.

143
00:07:22.175 --> 00:07:22.775
And remember,

144
00:07:22.775 --> 00:07:26.446
this is the template that we defined above
where this is the LineGraph user ID.

145
00:07:26.913 --> 00:07:29.916
Let me pass in Lance for that value.

146
00:07:31.584 --> 00:07:33.886
If we now call search on this namespace

147
00:07:33.886 --> 00:07:36.889
we can see the different items
that exist there.

148
00:07:37.390 --> 00:07:40.626
So we can see that we only have one item
because we've only remembered one piece

149
00:07:40.626 --> 00:07:44.897
of information,
and its value has this content:

150
00:07:44.931 --> 00:07:46.532
Jim is John Doe's friend.

151
00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:50.703
Notice the score here is none.

152
00:07:50.703 --> 00:07:53.706
That's because we haven't passed
in a query parameter.

153
00:07:54.040 --> 00:07:56.609
Let's pass one in, and let's see what

154
00:07:56.609 --> 00:07:59.612
the score is.

155
00:08:01.147 --> 00:08:03.115
If we pass in a query of Jim,

156
00:08:03.115 --> 00:08:06.752
we can now see that the score is 0.5
something.

157
00:08:08.054 --> 00:08:12.225
The score is calculated as cosine
similarity using the embedding model

158
00:08:12.492 --> 00:08:15.661
that we passed in when we created
the store in the first place.

159
00:08:17.396 --> 00:08:18.998
Now is a good time to pause.

160
00:08:18.998 --> 00:08:21.601
Use this response agent directly.

161
00:08:21.601 --> 00:08:25.271
Try querying it with different things
after to see if it remembers,

162
00:08:25.471 --> 00:08:28.474
and you can also inspect the store itself.

163
00:08:30.009 --> 00:08:30.977
Now, let's go on

164
00:08:30.977 --> 00:08:33.980
to creating the rest of the agent.

165
00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:37.016
So this should all look very similar
to the previous lesson,

166
00:08:37.016 --> 00:08:38.384
because not much is changing.

167
00:08:38.384 --> 00:08:41.387
We've mostly just added these tools
to the response agent.

168
00:08:42.255 --> 00:08:43.756
So first, we're creating the state.

169
00:08:43.756 --> 00:08:45.324
This is the same as before.

170
00:08:45.324 --> 00:08:48.160
We're making some imports, same as before.

171
00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:51.464
We're creating this triage
router node, same as before.

172
00:08:55.101 --> 00:08:57.537
We then create the email agent.

173
00:08:57.537 --> 00:09:00.306
This is the same as before
with two changes.

174
00:09:00.306 --> 00:09:03.409
First,
I've changed this to be response agent.

175
00:09:03.809 --> 00:09:06.512
So before, in previous lesson two
this was called agent.

176
00:09:06.512 --> 00:09:07.947
I've just updated the variable name.

177
00:09:07.947 --> 00:09:10.049
And so it's changed it here. Nothing.

178
00:09:10.049 --> 00:09:11.450
Nothing big.

179
00:09:11.450 --> 00:09:14.687
But next, I'm going to change
the compile step.

180
00:09:15.154 --> 00:09:17.990
So I'm going to pass in store
equals store.

181
00:09:17.990 --> 00:09:19.492
When I create the email agent.

182
00:09:19.492 --> 00:09:22.595
This is giving it access to that long-term
memory store

183
00:09:22.895 --> 00:09:25.798
that our memory tools are going to use.

184
00:09:25.798 --> 00:09:29.001
If I display the email agent
I get the same graph as before.

185
00:09:29.001 --> 00:09:31.971
So nothing's changed here.

186
00:09:32.305 --> 00:09:35.308
I can create this example email input.

187
00:09:35.408 --> 00:09:40.012
And then when I call the email agent
this is now also going to change slightly.

188
00:09:40.012 --> 00:09:44.183
I'm going to remember
to pass in my configuration this contains

189
00:09:44.183 --> 00:09:48.220
the LangGraph user ID that will
we will use in the memory tools.

190
00:09:49.188 --> 00:09:49.889
We can see that it

191
00:09:49.889 --> 00:09:53.559
runs and that at first classifies
that it should respond.

192
00:09:53.926 --> 00:09:56.929
And then it continues down that path.

193
00:09:58.731 --> 00:09:59.165
Once it's

194
00:09:59.165 --> 00:10:02.335
finished,
let's take a look at the list of messages

195
00:10:02.335 --> 00:10:05.605
that are in the agent's trajectory
so we can see what it does.

196
00:10:06.539 --> 00:10:08.474
So the first message
there is a human message.

197
00:10:08.474 --> 00:10:10.876
This is the input of the email.

198
00:10:10.876 --> 00:10:14.313
We then get back an AI message
where it's writing an email.

199
00:10:14.313 --> 00:10:18.117
So it has a tool call with this write
email and it's passing in

200
00:10:18.117 --> 00:10:18.985
and all these arguments.

201
00:10:18.985 --> 00:10:21.988
And so you can see the content
of the email that it writes here.

202
00:10:22.288 --> 00:10:25.491
We then get back a tool message saying
that it sent that email successfully.

203
00:10:26.125 --> 00:10:28.995
We then call the AI message again.

204
00:10:28.995 --> 00:10:31.130
And we see that
it calls the Managed Memory tool,

205
00:10:31.130 --> 00:10:35.267
and it creates this memory
with this content follow up needed.

206
00:10:35.301 --> 00:10:38.204
Alice Smith inquired
about missing API endpoints.

207
00:10:39.171 --> 00:10:42.008
We get back a
tool message that it created that memory,

208
00:10:42.008 --> 00:10:43.509
and then we get back the final response

209
00:10:43.509 --> 00:10:45.378
from the agent
that it's taking two actions.

210
00:10:45.378 --> 00:10:48.381
It sent a response to Alice,
and it's created a memory entry.

211
00:10:50.483 --> 00:10:51.784
So this is the agent

212
00:10:51.784 --> 00:10:54.787
with the semantic tools in place.

213
00:10:57.023 --> 00:10:59.825
Let's try out another example
that requires

214
00:10:59.825 --> 00:11:02.862
some of the memory that was saved
as part of this previous email.

215
00:11:03.963 --> 00:11:06.699
So let's pass in this new email from Alice
Smith as well.

216
00:11:06.699 --> 00:11:08.768
So same author and it says, hi John.

217
00:11:08.768 --> 00:11:11.771
Any update on my previous ask?

218
00:11:13.172 --> 00:11:16.108
When we invoke it,
we can see that it correctly classifies it

219
00:11:16.108 --> 00:11:19.111
as an email that it needs a response to,
and that continues

220
00:11:19.111 --> 00:11:22.114
from there.

221
00:11:22.882 --> 00:11:25.117
Let's take a look at the agent trajectory.

222
00:11:26.986 --> 00:11:29.989
So we can see that we
have the human message with the new email,

223
00:11:30.222 --> 00:11:33.559
and we can see that it now
first calls the search memory tool

224
00:11:34.293 --> 00:11:35.861
with this query Alice Smith

225
00:11:35.861 --> 00:11:38.197
previous request as communication.

226
00:11:38.197 --> 00:11:41.000
So it's trying to figure out
what information it has.

227
00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:45.671
It gets back this memory that it saved
before. We can see its logic.

228
00:11:46.372 --> 00:11:50.609
And then it calls this write email tool
and it writes this email.

229
00:11:51.110 --> 00:11:54.046
And we can see that in its response
it says: thanks for following up

230
00:11:54.046 --> 00:11:56.215
regarding the API endpoints documentation.

231
00:11:56.215 --> 00:12:00.619
So this is remember from before, even
though it's a completely separate email.

232
00:12:01.954 --> 00:12:05.658
We can see the tool message
that it sent a response to Alice.

233
00:12:06.092 --> 00:12:09.095
And then, we can see the final response.

234
00:12:10.329 --> 00:12:11.697
This is an example

235
00:12:11.697 --> 00:12:14.900
of how you can see that
the memory tools are saving relevant

236
00:12:14.900 --> 00:12:18.471
information, and then also searching
over it in the future.

237
00:12:20.272 --> 00:12:21.874
This is it for this lesson.

238
00:12:21.874 --> 00:12:24.944
So now is a great time to pause
and spend a few more minutes

239
00:12:24.944 --> 00:12:26.779
playing around with different emails,

240
00:12:26.779 --> 00:12:30.249
trying out different questions and seeing
if I can remember different facts.

241
00:12:31.283 --> 00:12:32.318
See you in the next lesson.

